WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:03.550
STEPHANIE WONG: Today, we're
talking about C++, Dataflow,

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:04.570
and BigQuery.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.180
I'm Stephanie Wong, and welcome
to "This Week in Cloud," where

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.920
we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.310
We are now building C++ client
libraries for all Google Cloud

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:14.440
services.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.680
Many of you are already using
the C++ libraries for Cloud

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.270
Storage and Bigtable.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:21.640
And we hear your
requests for more.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.160
We've just released a beta
version of the Cloud Spanner

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.790
C++ library.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.270
And we're actively working on
open source client libraries

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.210
for all of the remaining
Cloud services.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.480
Dataflow is Google Cloud's fully
managed batch and stream data

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:35.740
processing service.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:39.100
And it now includes throughput
and CPU utilization charts

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.660
that will help you diagnose and
remediate your pipeline issues.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.610
With better observability,
you can spend less time fixing

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:49.360
problems, and more time
getting value out of your data.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.810
BigQuery is a serverless, highly
scalable, and cost effective

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:54.220
Cloud data warehouse.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.340
It's designed to be flexible
and easy to use so that you make

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.560
a few decisions at the
start, and BigQuery

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.360
handles the work for you.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.460
In this post, one
of our eng leads

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.860
explains how zone
assignments work in BigQuery

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.340
and covers best practices
for organizing your data.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.650
Speaking of learning, we've
released the session guide

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:15.010
for Google Cloud Next 2020
in San Francisco on April 6th

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.030
through the 8th.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:19.030
Registration is open now, and
you can even plan your journey

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.840
by checking out the over 500
sessions we will be offering.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:23.787
Happy scrolling.

00:01:23.787 --> 00:01:25.870
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:28.090
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:29.510
Any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.010
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.540
We'll see you in the Cloud.

